{
  "rules": [
    "Strong upward-trend underestimation: when the last 10–15 days show a persistent rise (higher highs/higher lows), the model forecast level is systematically too low across the full 30d horizon. Apply an upward level shift that grows with horizon (small in days 1–5, larger by days 10–30).",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: the model reacts too slowly to regime changes (breakout from ~65–70 into ~75–83). If recent momentum is positive (e.g., 5–10d return > ~+3–5%), increase the forecast slope (steepen the path), not just the intercept.",
    "Mid-horizon ceiling effect: forecasts tend to flatten/mean-revert around ~68–71 even when realized prices continue trending up into the high-70s/80s. Remove/relax mean-reversion strength when momentum and volatility are elevated; allow higher terminal levels.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: errors are modest early but widen materially after ~day 7–10. Use a bias correction that increases with lead time (e.g., additive premium or multiplicative uplift ramping from near 0 at day 1 to maximum at day 30).",
    "Underprediction of spike magnitude: during sharp rallies, the model captures direction but not amplitude (peaks are too low). If recent daily ranges/volatility expand, scale up forecast moves (increase responsiveness) rather than smoothing them away."
  ]
}
